---
import AppLayout from "../layouts/AppLayout.astro";

/**
 * `/guide` — the help a person needs to use metis, in their words. The full
 * model (and its engineering rationale) stays at /ontology for whoever
 * builds on it; this page links there rather than repeating it.
 */
---

<AppLayout title="Guide">
  <div style="flex:1; min-height:0; overflow-y:auto;">
    <article class="guide">
      <h1>How metis works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Metis keeps one list of what your products can already do, so the next product starts from what
        exists instead of from zero. Four ideas carry everything else.
      </p>

      <dl class="terms">
        <div>
          <dt>Goal</dt>
          <dd>
            Why something exists. An <strong>org goal</strong> is the big aim; a <strong>product goal</strong>
            sits under one and says what a product is for. See them on <a href="/goals">Goals</a>; its
            <a href="/goals?view=network">Network</a> view links goals that rely on the same capabilities.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Capability</dt>
          <dd>
            Something we're able to do, named as a noun: <em>User authentication</em>, <em>Route planning</em>.
            Capabilities are shared: one capability can serve many products. They nest, so
            <em>Multi-factor auth</em> sits inside <em>User authentication</em>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Feature</dt>
          <dd>
            What a person gets, named as an action: <em>Sign in with your company account</em>. Each feature
            belongs to a product and does one or more capabilities. A feature's status is planned, in progress or done.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Technology</dt>
          <dd>
            Something we could buy or adopt instead of building, such as a library, service or vendor. A technology
            can cover the same capability a feature does; a capability's page compares the two once technology data exists.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/home">Home</a>: describe an idea as a few features and pick the capabilities each one needs.
          Metis says which ones we already have, which only other companies' products have, and which nobody has built yet.
          Save it as an idea to come back to. Home also scores the outside products you track, and lists the most
          reused capabilities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/products">Products</a>: one product at a time. <em>What it does</em> groups its features by
          capability; <em>Goals</em> says why it exists; <em>Plan</em> holds a spec, plan document and tickets, but
          only once someone asks for one. Products marked <em>external</em> belong to someone else; we record them
          to compare.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/journeys">Ideas &amp; journeys</a>: a journey is the path a person takes through features,
          possibly across products. An idea is a journey whose steps aren't built yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a>: the shared list, as a tree or most reused first, plus what needs
          attention. Each capability has one page: what it is, who uses it, why, and what depends on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/features">Features</a>: every feature, as a list or a graph of what needs what. Once a feature
          is planned but not started, a <em>Plan</em> tab appears and answers whole-graph questions: what's ready to
          start, the build order, and the smallest version that reaches a feature.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/chat">Chat</a>: ask any of this in plain words. When Chat proposes a change, nothing happens
          until you approve it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Getting products in</h2>
      <p>
        Metis only holds what your repos actually ship. A coding agent reads a repo and writes a seed file; you
        import it. On an empty instance, Home walks you through it; after that, it lives in
        <a href="/settings">Settings</a>. Technology and Stacks appear in the menu once technology data exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mapping a product by hand instead? Go in this order: create the product on
        <a href="/products">Products</a>, name the capabilities it uses on <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a>,
        add its features on <a href="/features">Features</a> and pick a capability for each, then say why:
        create a goal on <a href="/goals">Goals</a> and link it from each capability's page.
      </p>

      <h2>Reading the numbers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Live</strong>: one of our products does this today. <strong>Buildable</strong>: every capability
          it needs is ours. <strong>Partly known</strong>: everything named so far is ours, but the list is
          incomplete. <strong>Missing</strong>: it needs a capability we don't have. <strong>Not described</strong>:
          nobody has named what it needs yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          A capability's feature count means <strong>our features, including those of its sub-capabilities</strong>.
          Products we don't own are listed separately and never counted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Coverage is a lower bound: a capability nobody has identified yet can't count as one we have. Names match
          exactly; a near name is offered as "did you mean", never assumed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guesses</strong>: when nothing can confirm a link (no access to the feature, its code, its users or
          a public page that shows it), a seed file may still say a feature is <em>likely</em>, <em>possibly</em> or
          <em>on a hunch</em> built on a capability. Guesses count nowhere until an editor confirms one. Editors and
          admins see them on the feature, on the capability's page and under Capabilities, Needs attention.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hover a status or relationship word to see the exact value metis stores, the same one Chat, agents and
          logs use.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who can do what</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Viewer</strong>: reads everything.</li>
        <li><strong>Editor</strong>: also changes products, features, capabilities, goals and journeys, and imports seed files.</li>
        <li><strong>Admin</strong>: also manages users, AI models and raw data, from Settings.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>For AI agents</h2>
      <p>
        Agents can ask metis the same questions over MCP, read only: a write comes back refused, since writes are a
        person's action on the desktop.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Claude Code</strong>: install the plugin, which connects over SSE (below) and adds skills such as
          <code>metis-check-idea</code>.
          <div class="cmd">
            <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Claude Code commands"><code>/plugin marketplace add /path/to/metis
/plugin install metis@metis</code></pre>
            <button type="button" class="copy" hidden>Copy<span class="sr"> the Claude Code commands</span></button>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SSE</strong>: put a token in metis's <code>.env</code> as <code>METIS_MCP_SSE_TOKEN</code> (there
          is no default; the service won't start without it), then start the service from the metis checkout. It listens on
          <code>http://localhost:8001/sse</code>, on this machine only, and every request must send
          <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;that token&gt;</code>. The plugin reads the same value from
          <code>METIS_MCP_TOKEN</code>.
          <div class="cmd">
            <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="SSE commands"><code>echo "METIS_MCP_SSE_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" &gt;&gt; .env
docker compose --profile mcp up -d --build mcp</code></pre>
            <button type="button" class="copy" hidden>Copy<span class="sr"> the SSE commands</span></button>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>stdio</strong>, for an agent on the same machine, once the backend's <code>.venv</code> is installed
          (<code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code> in <code>backend</code>): it needs no bearer token, but with
          auth on it still signs in through Keycloak.
          <div class="cmd">
            <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="stdio command"><code>PYTHONPATH=/path/to/metis/backend \
MCP_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000 \
  /path/to/metis/backend/.venv/bin/python -m app.mcp</code></pre>
            <button type="button" class="copy" hidden>Copy<span class="sr"> the stdio command</span></button>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p role="status" class="sr" id="copied"></p>
      <p>
        Every tool and setting is in <code>docs/mcp.md</code> in the repository, and the plugin's in
        <code>plugin/README.md</code>.
      </p>

      <p class="more">
        Building on metis or extending its model? The full specification, with its engineering rationale, is in
        the <a href="/ontology">ontology reference</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</AppLayout>

<style>
  .guide {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-6) var(--space-12);
    color: var(--text-body);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
  }
  h1 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--text-xl); margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--text-lg); color: var(--text-heading); margin: 0 0 var(--space-6); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .terms { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); margin: 0; }
  .terms > div {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
  }
  dt { font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); color: var(--text-heading); }
  dd { margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty; }
  ul { padding-left: var(--space-5); margin: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  li { min-width: 0; } /* a long command scrolls in its box instead of widening the list */
  p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  ul + p { margin-top: var(--space-3); }
  /* A command too long for its box scrolls; a shade on each cut edge says so,
     and goes once that edge is scrolled to. Copy takes the whole of it. */
  .cmd { margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: var(--space-1); }
  pre {
    margin: 0; padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3); overflow-x: auto; align-self: stretch;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    background:
      linear-gradient(to right, var(--surface-card) 30%, transparent) left / 2.5rem 100% no-repeat local,
      linear-gradient(to left, var(--surface-card) 30%, transparent) right / 2.5rem 100% no-repeat local,
      linear-gradient(to right, var(--border-default), transparent) left / 1rem 100% no-repeat scroll,
      linear-gradient(to left, var(--border-default), transparent) right / 1rem 100% no-repeat scroll,
      var(--surface-card);
  }
  .copy {
    font: inherit; font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-accent); background: none; border: none;
    padding: 2px 0; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sr { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; margin: 0; }
  .more { margin-top: var(--space-8); color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .terms > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-1); }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Copy shows only where the clipboard works; it copies the command as written.
  const status = document.getElementById("copied");
  for (const button of document.querySelectorAll(".copy")) {
    if (!navigator.clipboard) continue;
    button.hidden = false;
    const label = button.firstChild;
    button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const pre = button.parentElement.querySelector("pre");
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(pre.innerText);
        label.textContent = "Copied";
        status.textContent = `Copied the ${pre.getAttribute("aria-label")}.`;
      } catch {
        label.textContent = "Copy failed";
        status.textContent = "Couldn't copy. Select the text instead.";
      }
      setTimeout(() => { label.textContent = "Copy"; }, 2000);
    });
  }
</script>
